  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nLow heights over the eastern Canada, and persistent ridging over the \nmountain west, will lead to wnw flow aloft into the Great Lakes thru \nthe forecast. The most active areas of wx will tend to be to our \nnorth (showery under much colder temps aloft) and south (MCS action \nalong a wavering frontal zone). Our wx remains comparatively\nquiet, but not absolutely so.\n\nIsolated convection is occurring early this morning, along a \nweak cold front making southward progress into the central \nlakes. MA few showers are moving east from the Cheboygan area, \nand there were a few showers earlier in ne WI. Guidance wants to\nbring a stray shower/storm into nw lower MI late overnight into\nearly this morning. Away from this, lower clouds and fog are \nforming with light winds and a moist BL. A larger area of \nconvection is moving into sw lower MI, and this may have some \nopportunity to grow upscale as it translates eastward. Models do\nallow the northern edge of this MCS to glance by Gladwin/Arenac\nCos this morning. Otherwise, for the afternoon, the Lk Huron \nlake breeze pushing into a synoptic nw breeze may trigger a few \nshowers/ storms in ne lower MI. Mainly quiet tonight, though \npatchy fog/stratus is again expected overnight. With very weak \nwinds Tuesday, lake breezes are likely to spawn spotty \nconvection, mainly in eastern upper and ne lower MI.\n\nBetter chance for showers/perhaps t-storms arrives late Tue \nnight into Wed, as weak low pressure and an associated shortwave\ncross lower MI. This gets replaced by a relatively strong/cool \nsurface high pressure, building into northern Ontario and \neventually northern Great Lakes late this week. Cooler temps but\ndry wx by Thu night-Fri. A more prominent shortwave and surface\nlow looks likely to push into the southern lakes Sat-Sun, \nreturning chances for rain. Max temps, after being near 80f \ntoday thru Wed, will lower into the 70s after that.",
